00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.670
Jack: So, let’s look at the
principles of compiled code and

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.520
interpreted code in more detail.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.299
With compiled code the program is
written in the source language.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.829
The code is then run through a
separate program called a compiler.

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:17.293
The compiler decomposes the original
code, parses it into components

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.259
and generates an executable file.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.389
This executable file is called a “binary”.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.679
The binary can then be executed
separately to run the program.

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:32.290
Leah: Since a binary must be generated
to run the program with compiled code,

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:36.790
this can make testing take longer, as
this must be done every time a change

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.810
is made which requires a new test.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:43.780
However, since the conversion to machine
code is done during the process of

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:49.740
generating an executable file, this means
that compiled codes can run faster as

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.780
this aspect of the work is already done.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.210
Jack: Before writing a binary,
the compiler will run a check for

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.320
syntax on the original source code.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.330
If there are errors, it
will flag a message and it

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:02.800
will not be able to compile.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.969
If there are no syntax errors, it
continues compiling the program

00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:08.619
to generate a binary file.

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:12.980
Compilers must convert source
code to a binary, which runs on

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.100
the specific operating system
that they are being compiled for.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:22.270
Leah: Compiled programs are therefore not
cross-platform, meaning that they cannot

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:28.589
be run directly from the source code
on incompatible operating systems/CPUs.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.920
However, it is possible to generate
binaries from the source code

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.460
which run on different systems
using the appropriate compiler.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:43.149
Examples of compiled languages
are C, C++, Objective-C

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.940
Jack: With Interpreted code the program
is written in the source language.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.510
When the program is executed, the
original source code is run line by

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.670
line through a separate program called
an interpreter in real time code.

00:01:58.309 --> 00:01:58.479
As

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.480
Leah: it runs, the interpreter
“translates” the source code into actions

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.440
which are then executable line by line.

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:09.959
Interpreted code can be easy
to debug, as the code can be

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.800
run whilst it is being written.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.549
This is often done using a
program called an Integrated

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.930
Development Environment [IDE].

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.849
This is a program used for development
where the code can be edited, but also has

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.010
a built-in interpreter for the language.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.950
The code can therefore be run within
the IDE [for example Spyder for Python,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.990
and Microsoft Visual Studio, which
has plug-ins for different languages].

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.370
Jack: When the code is run, the
interpreter runs the program line by

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.060
line, checking for syntax errors, then
performing the operations directly.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:49.160
If an error is encountered on any line,
the operation of the program will halt

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:53.760
in the state that it is in when the
error occurs, unless instructions on how

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.609
to deal with the error are written in.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:03:00.199
Leah: Interpreted code can essentially
be run on any operating system

00:03:00.309 --> 00:03:04.420
or CPU, providing that there is
an interpreter for that system.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.180
This means that the programs themselves
can be much more “portable”, but

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.909
that they may take longer to run
that compiled programs as they

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.920
are translated as they are run.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.440
Examples of Interpreted
languages are PHP, JavaScript.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.890
Jack: In order to get the
compatibility of interpretation

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.370
and the speed of normal compilation
[ahead-of-time], another method of

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.989
interpretation has been developed.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.410
This is called just-in-time [JIT]
compilation and it is where some

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:38.470
compilation occurs ahead of time through
an intermediate called byte code.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.890
Leah: The byte code converts as
much of the program into machine

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.369
code as is possible whilst still
being portable across platforms.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.400
The remaining parts are then
interpreted on a machine/operating

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.109
system-specific interpreter.

00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:58.900
Examples of this Hybrid are
Java, C#, VB.NET, Python.

